      // again on any later hash change. One shot, deliberately: re-scrolling on
      // every poll would yank a reader who has moved on.
      var anchored = false;

      // ── ONE CONVERSATION (CORE-117; Erin, via Darko: "a separate view of just one conversation window") ──
      // A #circle-<id> link (a card's "Open", the circle's own permalink, the World column's footer, an event page's circle, a feed trace)
      // shows THAT conversation and nothing else: the lists step aside, the one shared reader draws it, and the speaking area comes in
      // under it, in the page's flow. The office says which room the circle is in (`find.room`, the keyless ?circle= read: the circle's
      // own served identity, so it needs no list page to be held first). The page works out neither the room nor the circle: it asks.
      // Other talk, and the poll, never replace it: it lives outside the lists the poll paints. The poll never asks the office about it either
      // (CORE-156, SELECTED-ASKS below): new lines are offered from the list the page already holds, and one press asks for them. A refused or
      // missing circle says so, and is not an empty one.
      // A /conversations/?room=<id> link (CORE-177: an event page's "whole conversation") shows THAT room the same way: the office's NAMED room read (`?room=`, the keyless read that
      // resolves the id it is given, a historical one too, and answers with the room's own canonical header) says which room it is, and the same shared reader
      // draws it. It needs no list page to hold the room first: a room past the retained page, or recorded under another spelling, still opens. The id is opaque:
      // asked exactly as written, never rewritten, guessed or looked for in the lists. It is a QUERY, outside the fragment, so a bare #<id> (any id, `room-abc`
